./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:551:                  CLOP-DiT is a computational pipeline that generates synthetic single-cell gene expression profiles from structured biological descriptions (cell type, tissue, organism, marker genes, and disease context). It first learns to align text descriptions with real cell data in a shared mathematical space, then uses a diffusion model to generate new cell states matching a given description. The generated cells capture correct cell-type identity and marker gene patterns, but do not yet reproduce the full cell-to-cell variability seen in real single-cell experiments. This work demonstrates that text-guided single-cell generation is feasible as a proof of concept, opening directions for future simulation and hypothesis-generation tools in biology.",stable_include,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,yes,yes,yes,BiomedBERT text embeddings,text–cell alignment via contrastive aligner,conditional Diffusion Transformer (DiT) with flow matching,text_component_boundary,The abstract presents a generative model for single-cell generation conditioned on structured biological descriptions using BiomedBERT text embeddings and scGPT. Text component is unclear because the input is structured metadata fields rather than free-form natural language.,"The abstract presents a new pipeline (CLOP-DiT) for single-cell generation conditioned on structured biological metadata; the text component is unclear because the conditioning fields are categorical labels rather than natural-language text, and the contrastive alignment uses text embeddings but the input is structured, not free-form text.","The abstract describes a conditional Diffusion Transformer that generates single-cell transcriptomic profiles from structured biological text descriptions, providing clear evidence of a generative model with broad pretraining across multiple datasets.",The abstract presents a generative model for single-cell generation conditioned on structured biological descriptions using BiomedBERT text embeddings and scGPT. Text component is unclear because the input is structured metadata fields rather than free-form natural language.,CLOP-DiT: Structured-Metadata-Conditioned Single-Cell Latent Generation via Contrastive Language-Omics Pretraining and Diffusion Transformers,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:576:1818,CaLMFlow: Volterra Flow Matching using Causal Language Models,"We introduce CaLMFlow (Causal Language Models for Flow Matching), a novel framework that casts flow matching as a Volterra integral equation (VIE), leveraging the power of large language models (LLMs) for continuous data generation. CaLMFlow enables the direct application of LLMs to learn complex flows by formulating flow matching as a sequence modeling task, bridging discrete language modeling and continuous generative modeling. Our method implements tokenization across space and time, thereby solving a VIE over these domains. This approach enables efficient handling of high-dimensional data and outperforms ODE solver-dependent methods like conditional flow matching (CFM). We demonstrate CaLMFlow's effectiveness on synthetic and real-world data, including single-cell perturbation response prediction, showcasing its ability to incorporate textual context and generalize to unseen conditions. Our results highlight LLM-driven flow matching as a promising paradigm in generative modeling, offering improved scalability, flexibility, and context-awareness.",stable_include,CaLMFlow: Volterra Flow Matching using Causal Language Models,"We introduce CaLMFlow (Causal Language Models for Flow Matching), a novel framework that casts flow matching as a Volterra integral equation (VIE), leveraging the power of large language models (LLMs) for continuous data generation. CaLMFlow enables the direct application of LLMs to learn complex flows by formulating flow matching as a sequence modeling task, bridging discrete language modeling and continuous generative modeling. Our method implements tokenization across space and time, thereby solving a VIE over these domains. This approach enables efficient handling of high-dimensional data and outperforms ODE solver-dependent methods like conditional flow matching (CFM). We demonstrate CaLMFlow's effectiveness on synthetic and real-world data, including single-cell perturbation response prediction, showcasing its ability to incorporate textual context and generalize to unseen conditions. Our results highlight LLM-driven flow matching as a promising paradigm in generative modeling, offering improved scalability, flexibility, and context-awareness.",stable_include,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,yes,unclear,none,none,continuous data generation; flow matching,text_component_boundary,"Abstract presents a novel flow matching framework using LLMs for continuous data generation, with application to single-cell perturbation. Text component and text-bio bridge are unclear because the abstract only mentions 'textual context' without explicit natural-language input/output or alignment. Generative model is clear.","The abstract presents a novel generative framework using LLMs for flow matching with single-cell perturbation prediction, but the nature of 'textual context' is ambiguous, leaving text component and text-bio bridge unclear.","The abstract presents CaLMFlow, a generative framework using causal language models for flow matching, with explicit mention of continuous data generation and application to single-cell perturbation prediction.","Abstract presents a novel flow matching framework using LLMs for continuous data generation, with application to single-cell perturbation. Text component and text-bio bridge are unclear because the abstract only mentions 'textual context' without explicit natural-language input/output or alignment. Generative model is clear.",CaLMFlow: Volterra Flow Matching using Causal Language Models,"We introduce CaLMFlow (Causal Language Models for Flow Matching), a novel framework that casts flow matching as a Volterra integral equation (VIE), leveraging the power of large language models (LLMs) for continuous data generation. CaLMFlow enables the direct application of LLMs to learn complex flows by formulating flow matching as a sequence modeling task, bridging discrete language modeling and continuous generative modeling. Our method implements tokenization across space and time, thereby solving a VIE over these domains. This approach enables efficient handling of high-dimensional data and outperforms ODE solver-dependent methods like conditional flow matching (CFM). We demonstrate CaLMFlow's effectiveness on synthetic and real-world data, including single-cell perturbation response prediction, showcasing its ability to incorporate textual context and generalize to unseen conditions. Our results highlight LLM-driven flow matching as a promising paradigm in generative modeling, offering improved scalability, flexibility, and context-awareness.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,unclear,,,,,"CaLMFlow is a primary model paper that uses causal language models for flow matching, applied to single-cell perturbation response prediction with textual context, satisfying all criteria. | Introduces CaLMFlow, a flow matching framework using causal language models for continuous data generation, demonstrated on single-cell perturbation response prediction, indicating a generative text-bio model.","CaLMFlow is a primary model paper that uses causal language models for flow matching, applied to single-cell perturbation response prediction with textual context, satisfying all criteria.","Introduces CaLMFlow, a flow matching framework using causal language models for continuous data generation, demonstrated on single-cell perturbation response prediction, indicating a generative text-bio model.",,CaLMFlow: Volterra Flow Matching using Causal Language Models,"We introduce CaLMFlow (Causal Language Models for Flow Matching), a novel framework that casts flow matching as a Volterra integral equation (VIE), leveraging the power of large language models (LLMs) for continuous data generation. CaLMFlow enables the direct application of LLMs to learn complex flows by formulating flow matching as a sequence modeling task, bridging discrete language modeling and continuous generative modeling. Our method implements tokenization across space and time, thereby solving a VIE over these domains. This approach enables efficient handling of high-dimensional data and outperforms ODE solver-dependent methods like conditional flow matching (CFM). We demonstrate CaLMFlow's effectiveness on synthetic and real-world data, including single-cell perturbation response prediction, showcasing its ability to incorporate textual context and generalize to unseen conditions. Our results highlight LLM-driven flow matching as a promising paradigm in generative modeling, offering improved scalability, flexibility, and context-awareness.",stable_include,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,yes,unclear,incorporate textual context,incorporate textual context for single-cell perturbation response prediction,flow matching for continuous data generation,thin_abstract_boundary,"CaLMFlow is a primary model paper presenting a flow matching framework using LLMs for continuous data generation, with single-cell perturbation response prediction. However, the abstract does not clearly describe a natural-language text component or explicit text-bio alignment; both text_component and text_bio_bridge remain unclear. Scope reviewer flagged these as unclear, while architecture reviewer did not address them.","The abstract presents a new flow matching framework using LLMs for continuous data generation applied to single-cell perturbation, but does not clearly specify natural-language text input/output or a substantive text-bio bridge beyond vague 'textual context'.","The abstract presents CaLMFlow, a novel generative framework using causal language models for flow matching, with applications including single-cell perturbation prediction. It describes a generative mechanism (flow matching) and is not a wrapper or review.","CaLMFlow is a primary model paper presenting a flow matching framework using LLMs for continuous data generation, with single-cell perturbation response prediction. However, the abstract does not clearly describe a natural-language text component or explicit text-bio alignment; both text_component and text_bio_bridge remain unclear. Scope reviewer flagged these as unclear, while architecture reviewer did not address them.",CaLMFlow: Volterra Flow Matching using Causal Language Models,"We introduce CaLMFlow (Causal Language Models for Flow Matching), a novel framework that casts flow matching as a Volterra integral equation (VIE), leveraging the power of large language models (LLMs) for continuous data generation. CaLMFlow enables the direct application of LLMs to learn complex flows by formulating flow matching as a sequence modeling task, bridging discrete language modeling and continuous generative modeling. Our method implements tokenization across space and time, thereby solving a VIE over these domains. This approach enables efficient handling of high-dimensional data and outperforms ODE solver-dependent methods like conditional flow matching (CFM). We demonstrate CaLMFlow's effectiveness on synthetic and real-world data, including single-cell perturbation response prediction, showcasing its ability to incorporate textual context and generalize to unseen conditions. Our results highlight LLM-driven flow matching as a promising paradigm in generative modeling, offering improved scalability, flexibility, and context-awareness.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents a new framework (CaLMFlow) using LLMs and explicitly mentions applying it to single‑cell perturbation response prediction, indicating a biological modality, a substantive text component, and a text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents CaLMFlow, a novel framework that leverages large language models for continuous data generation via flow matching, indicating a primary generative model with foundation-model characteristics.","The abstract presents a new framework (CaLMFlow) using LLMs and explicitly mentions applying it to single‑cell perturbation response prediction, indicating a biological modality, a substantive text component, and a text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents CaLMFlow, a novel framework that leverages large language models for continuous data generation via flow matching, indicating a primary generative model with foundation-model characteristics.",,CaLMFlow: Volterra Flow Matching using Causal Language Models,"We introduce CaLMFlow (Causal Language Models for Flow Matching), a novel framework that casts flow matching as a Volterra integral equation (VIE), leveraging the power of large language models (LLMs) for continuous data generation. CaLMFlow enables the direct application of LLMs to learn complex flows by formulating flow matching as a sequence modeling task, bridging discrete language modeling and continuous generative modeling. Our method implements tokenization across space and time, thereby solving a VIE over these domains. This approach enables efficient handling of high-dimensional data and outperforms ODE solver-dependent methods like conditional flow matching (CFM). We demonstrate CaLMFlow's effectiveness on synthetic and real-world data, including single-cell perturbation response prediction, showcasing its ability to incorporate textual context and generalize to unseen conditions. Our results highlight LLM-driven flow matching as a promising paradigm in generative modeling, offering improved scalability, flexibility, and context-awareness.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The paper introduces a novel LLM‑based flow‑matching model applied to single‑cell data and explicitly mentions using textual context, indicating a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents CaLMFlow, a novel framework that uses large language models to perform flow matching and generate continuous data, indicating a genuine generative architecture and leveraging a foundation model.","The paper introduces a novel LLM‑based flow‑matching model applied to single‑cell data and explicitly mentions using textual context, indicating a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents CaLMFlow, a novel framework that uses large language models to perform flow matching and generate continuous data, indicating a genuine generative architecture and leveraging a foundation model.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:581:                We introduce Cell2Sentence (C2S), a novel method to directly adapt large language models to a biological context, specifically single-cell transcriptomics. By transforming gene expression data into “cell sentences,” C2S bridges the gap between natural language processing and biology. We demonstrate cell sentences enable the fine-tuning of language models for diverse tasks in biology, including cell generation, complex cell-type annotation, and direct data-driven text generation. Our experiments reveal that GPT-2, when fine-tuned with C2S, can generate biologically valid cells based on cell type inputs, and accurately predict cell types from cell sentences. This illustrates that language models, through C2S fine-tuning, can acquire a significant understanding of single-cell biology while maintaining robust text generation capabilities. C2S offers a flexible, accessible framework to integrate natural language processing with transcriptomics, utilizing existing models and libraries for a wide range of biological applications.",stable_include,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,yes,direct data-driven text generation,transforming gene expression data into 'cell sentences',"cell generation, direct data-driven text generation",text_component_boundary,"The abstract presents Cell2Sentence as a method to adapt LLMs to single-cell transcriptomics using cell sentences (biological tokens), not natural-language text. While the model is generative and uses GPT-2, the text component is not clearly natural language, so text_component_present=no and text_bio_bridge_present=no, leading to exclusion EC2_no_text_component. The paper is a primary model paper, not a wrapper, because it introduces a fine-tuning method.","The abstract describes transforming gene expression data into 'cell sentences,' which are biological token sequences, not natural-language text. Therefore, the candidate model lacks a substantive text/language component.","The paper fine-tunes an existing generative model (GPT-2) for biological tasks, making it an application wrapper rather than a new foundational model.","The abstract presents Cell2Sentence as a method to adapt LLMs to single-cell transcriptomics using cell sentences (biological tokens), not natural-language text. While the model is generative and uses GPT-2, the text component is not clearly natural language, so text_component_present=no and text_bio_bridge_present=no, leading to exclusion EC2_no_text_component. The paper is a primary model paper, not a wrapper, because it introduces a fine-tuning method.",Cell2Sentence: teaching large language models the language of biology,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:582:                We introduce Cell2Sentence (C2S), a novel method to directly adapt large language models to a biological context, specifically single-cell transcriptomics. By transforming gene expression data into “cell sentences,” C2S bridges the gap between natural language processing and biology. We demonstrate cell sentences enable the fine-tuning of language models for diverse tasks in biology, including cell generation, complex cell-type annotation, and direct data-driven text generation. Our experiments reveal that GPT-2, when fine-tuned with C2S, can generate biologically valid cells based on cell type inputs, and accurately predict cell types from cell sentences. This illustrates that language models, through C2S fine-tuning, can acquire a significant understanding of single-cell biology while maintaining robust text generation capabilities. C2S offers a flexible, accessible framework to integrate natural language processing with transcriptomics, utilizing existing models and libraries for a wide range of biological applications.",stable_include,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,yes,none,none,can generate biologically valid cells,biological_token_only_boundary,"The abstract converts gene expression data into 'cell sentences' which are biological tokens, not natural-language text, so text_component_present is no, leading to exclusion under EC2_no_text_component.",The abstract describes transforming gene expression into 'cell sentences' (biological tokens) for fine-tuning LLMs; no natural-language text component or text-bio bridge is present.,"The paper fine-tunes GPT-2 for single-cell transcriptomics tasks, including cell generation, making it an application wrapper around an existing generative model rather than a new foundational model.","The abstract converts gene expression data into 'cell sentences' which are biological tokens, not natural-language text, so text_component_present is no, leading to exclusion under EC2_no_text_component.",Cell2Sentence: teaching large language models the language of biology,"Abstract
