./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:368:{"cluster_id": 3853, "title": "Discrete Diffusion for Single-Cell Gene Expression Modeling", "title_normalized": "discrete diffusion for singlecell gene expression modeling", "doi": NaN, "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "A\n                  bstract\n                \n                \n                  Current generative modeling of single-cell transcriptomics relies on continuous latent representations, transforming inherently discrete and sparse gene counts into continuous space. We propose Discrete Cell Models (DCM), a diffusion-based framework that learns cellular representations directly in the discrete domain. Our framework supports both unconditional and conditional generation, allowing for precise modeling of complex biological scenarios such as cell-type-specific transcriptional responses to genetic perturbations. We demonstrate that DCM scales effectively and achieves strong performance against current state-of-the-art methods, including scVI, CPA, STATE, scGPT, and scLDM. On the Dentate Gyrus benchmark, DCM achieves a 5-fold improvement in\n                  MMD\n                  2\n                  RBF\n                  and a nearly 2-fold improvement in\n                  W\n                  2\n                  distance, over the leading continuous diffusion baseline (scLDM). On the conditional Replogle perturbation benchmark, DCM sets a new state of the art on\n                  W\n                  2\n                  distance while remaining competitive on\n                  MMD\n                  2\n                  RBF\n                  . Together, these results establish discrete diffusion as a promising direction for foundational models of cellular biology.", "authors": "['S Bhattacharya', 'C Gensbigler', '